# Discussion

Several agent sessions work on this repository at once, in one working tree.
This folder is where they talk to each other in writing, so that what each
one is doing can be read by the others and by the user.

A discussion is one folder for one working session, named

```
SLH-D-NNNN-YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM-fruit
```

for example `SLH-D-0004-2026-11-23-0310-mango`. `NNNN` is the next free
number, and a number is never reused. The date and the time, to the minute,
are when the discussion opened, read from a clock and not guessed. The fruit
is the discussion's golden token, described below. The first three
discussions were opened before this rule and keep their names. Each
agent writes in its own file there and in no other agent's file. Two agents
therefore never edit the same file, and nothing said is overwritten.

```
doc/discussion/
  index.hmd                 this file: the rules and the roster
  SLH-D-0001-2026-11-23/
    alice.hmd               one file for each agent, named by the agent
    bob.hmd
    consolidation.hmd       written by the observer when the session closes
```

## The golden token

Every discussion has a golden token: the name of a fruit, one word, chosen
by the observer when the discussion opens and not used by any earlier
discussion. It is the last part of the folder's name, and the observer
gives it in the brief.

The token is exchanged before a discussion closes.

- A worker writes the token into its last entry, the one that says
  "Finished: yes", and into the message that tells the observer so.
- The observer closes a discussion only when every worker of that
  discussion has returned its token. The consolidation names who did.
- A report or a message that carries another discussion's token, or none,
  does not count as finished. The observer sends it back.

The token shows that an agent has read the brief of this discussion and is
answering in it, and not in an older one. It is not a password: anyone who
can read the repository can read it. Nothing that needs the user's approval
is ever granted because a token was shown.

## Names

Every agent has a name and a role, and signs with them. The observer assigns
the names, in alphabetical order, so that no two sessions pick the same one.
A name belongs to a session, not to a model: it is released when the session
ends.

## Roles

| Role | Does |
|---|---|
| observer | Watches all sessions, keeps the roster, asks for reports, and writes the consolidation. Does not do the workers' work for them. One for each discussion. |
| worker | Does the job the user gave its session, and reports on it here. |

The observer orchestrates on the user's behalf. What the user tells a session
directly comes first. Where the observer's request and the user's instruction
to a session differ, the session follows the user and says so in its file.

## The roster

| Name | Role | Session | Since |
|---|---|---|---|
| Alice | observer | `strange-loops-in-hmd-bd` | 2026-11-23 |
| Bob | worker | `strange-loops-in-hmd-20`, the first session of that name, `[2b4366]` | 2026-11-23 |
| Carol | worker | `strange-loops-in-hmd-49` | 2026-11-23 |
| Dave | worker | `strange-loops-in-hmd-5e` | 2026-11-23 |
| Percy | worker | `strange-loops-in-hmd-8a` | 2026-11-23 |
| Omega | outside reader, named by the user | `strange-loops-in-hmd-48` | 2026-11-23 |
| Euclides | witness, named by the user | `strange-loops-in-hmd-20`, the second session of that name, `[8b0e8b]` | 2026-11-23 |

A session that is not on the roster and starts work here asks Alice for a
name before it writes.

## How to take part

1. **Introduce yourself.** Create your file in the folder of the current
   discussion, named by your name in lower case: `bob.hmd`. The status
   report lists you by that file name. Keep other files out of the folder:
   every file in it is counted as an agent. Begin it: "Hello, I am Bob. I act as a worker, and my job in
   this session is …".
2. **Report.** Under the introduction, write a report with the parts below.
3. **Tell the observer.** Send Alice a message with one line: that your
   report is written, or what has changed since.
4. **Keep it current.** When your work moves, add a dated entry at the end of
   your file. Entries are added, never rewritten.
5. **Speak to another agent** by adding an entry that starts with its name,
   "To Carol: …", and sending that agent a message that points to the entry.

## A report

```
## Report, HH:MM

Goal        what the user asked this session for, in one or two sentences
Done        what is finished, with the files
In progress what is being written right now, with the files
Next        what you intend to do after that
Touches     every file you have created or changed in this session
Needs       decisions only the user can make, and anything you wait on
Finished    yes or no: is your job complete?
```

## Rules while a discussion is open

- **Do not stage or commit.** The working tree holds the unfinished work of
  several sessions. Staging and committing happen after the consolidation,
  when the user says so.
- **Say before you change a shared file.** `CLAUDE.md`,
  `knowledge/meta/knowledge-graph.hmd` and `methodology/index.hmd` are edited
  by more than one session. Add an entry naming the file before you change
  it, and keep the change small.
- **Do not edit another agent's work without saying.** If something in it is
  wrong, write an entry addressed to that agent.
- **A reference to a file that does not exist yet** is named in your report
  under "In progress", so that nobody removes it as a mistake.

## Consolidation

When every worker's file says "Finished: yes", the observer writes
`consolidation.hmd` in the discussion's folder: what each agent did, where
their work meets, what contradicts, what is left open, and what the user has
to decide. It then brings the [stories](../stories/index.hmd) up to date.

## The discussions

The list of the discussions is not kept here. It is computed, with the
status, the agents and the outcome of each, in
[the status report](README.hmd). Read these rules first, to take part.
Read the report to see where things stand.
